{"level":30,"time":1767417185973,"pid":95960,"hostname":"liangweijiedeMacBook-Pro.local","currentTitle":"Coffee","summary":"Coffee is a beverage brewed from roasted, ground coffee beans. Darkly colored, bitter, and slightly acidic, coffee has a stimulating effect on humans, primarily due to its caffeine content, but decaffeinated coffee is also commercially available. There are also various coffee substitutes. \nCoffee production begins when the seeds from coffee cherries (the Coffea plant's fruits) are separated to produce unroasted green coffee beans. The \"beans\" are roasted and then ground into fine particles. Coffee is brewed from the ground roasted beans, which are typically steeped in hot water before being filtered out. It is usually served hot, although chilled or iced coffee is common. Coffee can be prepared and presented in a variety of ways (e.g., espresso, French press, caffè latte, or already-brewed canned coffee). Sugar, sugar substitutes, milk, and cream are often added to mask the bitter taste or enhance the flavor.\nThough coffee has become a global commodity, it has a long history tied closely to food traditions around the Red Sea. The earliest credible reports of coffee drinking pertain to the plant's use among the Sufis of Yemen in the middle of the 15th century. Up to the end of the 17th century, most of the world's coffee was imported from Yemen. But as the beverage gained in popularity, coffee started to be cultivated in Java in the 17th century, as well as in the Americas from the 18th century onward.\nThe two most commonly grown coffee bean types are C. arabica and C. robusta. Coffee plants are cultivated in over 70 countries, primarily in the equatorial regions of the Americas, Southeast Asia, the Indian subcontinent, and Africa. Green, unroasted coffee is traded as an agricultural commodity. The global coffee industry is worth $495.50 billion, as of 2023. In 2023, Brazil was the leading grower of coffee beans, producing 31% of the world's total, followed by Vietnam. While coffee sales reach billions of dollars annually worldwide, coffee farmers disproportionately live in poverty. Critics of the coffee industry have pointed to its negative impact on the environment, including clearing of land for coffee growing and water use.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coffee","goal":"French Revolution","history":[],"depth":0,"links":["Abbie Cornish","Abd al-Qadir al-Jaziri","Abu Bakr al-Razi","Abyssinia","Acacia","Academic Press","Adal Sultanate","Aden","Adenosine receptor","Adventist Health Studies","AeroPress","Affogato","Age of Sail","Ajam","Al-Hawi","Al-Shihr","Alan Desmond","Albizia","Alfredo Bowman","Alton Brown","American Birding Association","American Journal of Epidemiology","American Revolution","American University","American yellow warbler","Americas","Andrea Nguyen","Anorectic","Antioxidant","Arabic coffee","Arabinogalactans","Aramaic","Arnold Schwarzenegger","Ashwagandha","Asian palm civet","Atlantic Forest","Average Joe","Avicenna","BBC","BMJ","B vitamins","Baba Budan","Balliol College, Oxford","Bar (unit)","Barista","Barley tea","Barleycup","Basic Books","Battle of Vienna","Bean","Beaten coffee","Beliefnet","Benguet coffee","Bibcode (identifier)","Bica (coffee)","Bicerin","Bikini barista","Bioeconomy","Black Coffee (disambiguation)","Black Russian","Blade grinder","Bloomberg News","Boma Plateau","Bosporus strait","Boston Tea Party","Bourbon coffee","Brazil","Break (work)","Brenda Davis","Brendan Brazier","Brewed coffee","Brosimum alicastrum","Burr mill","CAB International","Cafestol","Caffeic acid","Caffeine","Caffeine dependence","Caffè Americano","Caffè corretto","Caffè crema","Caffè d'orzo","Caffè macchiato","Caffè mocha","Caffè sospeso","Café Touba","Café au lait","Café com cheirinho","Café con leche","Café de olla","Cairo","Caldwell Esselstyn","Calum Harris","Canned coffee","Cappuccino","Carajillo","Caramelization","Carleigh Bodrug","Caro (drink)","Cash crop","Cassia (genus)","Casuar